{{also|setback}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-set back.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|set<,,set> back}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[delay]] or [[obstruct]].
#: {{cot|en|hold back}}
#: {{ux|en|I expect it will '''set''' us '''back''' by a day or so, but I think a side trip will be worthwhile.}}
#: {{ux|en|Having the mindset that you "can't" reach your goals is only going to '''set''' you '''back'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|IV|51|“Well,” I answered, at first with uncertainty, then with inspiration, “he would do splendidly to lead your cotillon, if you think of having one.” &para; “So you do not dance, Mr. Crocker?” &para; I was somewhat '''set back''' by her perspicuity.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[remove]] from or allow [[distance]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Set''' it '''back''' from the road by twenty or thirty feet.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|title=AA Book of British Villages|publisher=Drive Publications Ltd|page=273, about [[Mallwyd]]|text=Roads from [[Machynlleth]], [[Dolgellau]] and [[Welshpool]] meet at [[Mallwyd]], but this tiny village is '''set back''' from passing traffic and retains its atmosphere of charm and tranquillity.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[install]] or position behind a boundary or surface, or in a [[recess]].
#: {{cot|en|inset#Verb}}
#: {{ux|en|The statue was '''set back''' in a niche.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[cost]] [[money]].
#: {{ux|en|How much do you suppose that fancy dress '''set''' her '''back'''?}}
# To [[reverse]], go backwards.
#: {{cot|en|back out|back up|go back}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1939|month=December|author=John D. Hewitt|title=Some Notable British Main Lines: II. Salisbury and Exeter, S.R.|journal=Railway Magazine|page=412|text=Before the platforms were extended up to the actual junction, branch trains had to '''set back''' into a bay.}}

